// Pseudo-test: hash payload for idempotency validation
const crypto = require('crypto');
function hashPayload(payload) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256')
    .update(JSON.stringify(payload))
    .digest('hex');
}

// Validate idempotent upserts by comparing hash signatures
```

## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design end-to-end, multi-tenant privacy-conscious tests for streaming data, including isolation, schema evolution, and canary risk.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Data privacy and retention
- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Streaming backpressure and performance
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple data leakage check
function checkIsolation(records) {
  const seenTenants = new Set();
  for (const r of records) {
    if (seenTenants.has(r.tenantId)) return false;
    seenTenants.add(r.tenantId);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generate tenant-scoped synthetic data with privacy constraints?
- What instrumentation would you add to verify no cross-tenant leakage under burst load?

## Why This Is Asked

Webhooks are central to reliable integrations in payments; testing ensures resilience to retries, dedup, and partial failures, which beginner testers can demonstrate through practical CI plans.

## Key Concepts

- At-least-once delivery
- Idempotency
- Retry strategies and jitter
- End-to-end testing with mocks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: webhook sink with idempotency key check
const deliveries = new Map();
function deliver(ctx) {
  const key = ctx.idempotencyKey;
  if (deliveries.has(key)) return 'duplicate';
  deliveries.set(key, true);
  return 'delivered';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test out-of-order deliveries?
- How would you simulate a non-idempotent endpoint handle correctly?

